{
  "id": "KY-HB-534-2026",
  "scope": "state:ky",
  "title": "HB 534 (2026 Regular Session): AN ACT relating to elections.",
  "urls": [
    "https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/record/26RS/hb534.html",
    "https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/record/26RS/hb534/vote_history.pdf"
  ],
  "version": "other",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated.\n(b_no_id_remedy) not stated.\n(c_mail_ballot_id) not stated.\n(d_proof_of_citizenship)\n(section) Section 3(1), new section of KRS Chapter 116\n(quote) The State Board of Elections is authorized to enter into agreements with agencies of the federal government to identify individuals who are not citizens of the United States and who are registered to vote in this state.\n(section) Section 3(4)\n(quote) An individual identified as a noncitizen on the roster of qualified voters shall not cast a ballot until his or her United States citizenship is verified. To verify his or her United States citizenship, he or she shall present one (1) or more of the following at the office of his or her county clerk or at a voting location:\n(section) Section 3(4)(b)-(e)\n(quote) (b) An undamaged United States passport that is or was valid for ten (10) years for adults or five (5) years for children under sixteen (16) and does not contain a United States National endorsement; (c) A Consular Report of Birth Abroad or Certification of Birth; (d) A Certificate of Naturalization; or (e) A Certificate of Citizenship.\n(section) Section 3(6)\n(quote) If an individual identified as a noncitizen on the roster of qualified voters appears at a voting location to vote, the election officer shall allow the voter to cast a provisional ballot only after the voter has attested under penalty of perjury that he or she is a United States citizen on a form provided by the State Board of Elections.\n(section) Section 3(1), new section of KRS Chapter 116\n(quote) The State Board of Elections shall compare on a monthly basis the statewide voter registration system against the Systematic Alien Verification for Entitlements (SAVE) Program, or its successor program, operated by the United States Department of Homeland Security, to identify individuals who are not citizens of the United States who are registered to vote in this state.\n(section) Section 3(2)(a)\n(quote) Cause the removal of the name of that person from the voter registration records it maintains, except that a voter's name shall not be removed during the period of time the registration books are closed for any primary, general, or special election\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) Section 3(2)(b)1-2\n(quote) The federal agency shall be provided with a copy of the roster of all qualified registered voters within the state maintained by the State Board of Elections pursuant to KRS 117.025; 2. The copy of the roster that is provided to the federal agency shall include only the name, date of birth, and last four (4) digits of the Social Security number, if available, of registered voters within the state;\n(section) Section 3(3)(a)\n(quote) Mark the individual's registration in the roster of all qualified registered voters maintained pursuant to KRS 117.025(3)(a) in such a way that indicates on the voter registration system and any e-poll book that the individual has been identified as a noncitizen\n(section) Section 3(7)\n(quote) An individual identified as a noncitizen on the roster of qualified voters shall be purged from the roster of qualified voters if the individual does not verify his or her United States citizenship before the day after the date of the second general election for federal office that occurs after the individual was identified as a noncitizen on the roster of qualified voters.\n(section) Section 3(8)\n(quote) The voter registration of all individuals who are identified as being a noncitizen through an agreement authorized by this section shall be forwarded by the State Board of Elections to the Unit of Election Investigations and Security within the Office of the Attorney General.\n(other) Section 1 (KRS 27A.070): Administrative Office of the Courts list changes (felony convictions, jury-duty citizenship excusals) sent to the elections board.\n(other) Section 2 (KRS 83A.040): special election when most of a city legislative body's seats are vacated by an election error; incumbents stay until a contest ends.\n(other) Section 3 (new KRS 116 section): federal-agreement check for noncitizens on the voter rolls (see slot d); the introduced bill instead had a monthly SAVE comparison with removal within 5 days.\n(other) Section 4 (KRS 116.113): felony-conviction removal lists include people whose cases are on appeal.\n(other) Section 5 (KRS 117.125): voting equipment purchased after the Act may not use barcodes, QR codes or other encrypted markings to represent or tabulate votes (line 662 of the saved text).\n(other) Sections 6-7 (KRS 117.155, 117.205): cross-reference renumbers.\n(other) Section 8 (KRS 117.383): hand-to-eye recount rule (scanner and race selected need at least 10 votes).\n(other) Section 9 (KRS 118.176): costs recoverable by a person who successfully challenges a candidate's bona fides.\n(other) Section 10 (KRS 118.405): candidate name appearing more than once on a ballot (reprinted).\n(other) Section 11 (new KRS 118A section): what judicial candidates may and may not say about party.\n(other) Section 12 (KRS 120.165): special election when a majority of offices of a legislative body is vacant; costs of a successful contest.\n(other) Sections 13-18 (KRS 121.x): campaign-finance rules (party executive committee, contributions, registry); the 'foreign national' definition ('not a citizen or lawful permanent resident') is a campaign-finance definition, not a voting rule.\n(other) version: orig_bill (as introduced, 13 sections): slot d = monthly comparison of the whole statewide voter list against the federal SAVE program; non-citizens removed from the rolls within 5 days (not during book closing) and sent to the Attorney General. No document step for the voter. Also carries new KRS 117 'cast vote record' section and other items later dropped.\n(other) version: bill (furthest = as passed House on 2026-03-05, 'Committee Substitute (2) and Committee Amendment (1-title)', header 26 RS HB 534/GA): slot d is rewritten. Federal-agency agreements (SAVE not named); flagged voters are marked, mailed a notice, cannot cast a ballot until they show a listed document at the county clerk or a voting location, may vote a provisional ballot only after a citizenship attestation, and are purged after the second federal general election. The Senate never acted, so this is not law. HCS 1, HCS 2 and floor amendments 10 to 12 are now saved (see lines below).\n(other) version: HCS1 (House committee substitute 1, 26 RS HB 534/HCS 1, saved 2026-11-11): carries slot d. Section 3 is already the rewritten check: federal-agency agreements, flagged voters may not vote until they show a listed document at the county clerk or a voting location. It also lets a flagged voter who shows documents at a voting location vote after the officer copies them, and keeps the new 'cast vote record' section (KRS 117). Against the introduced bill the SAVE monthly comparison is gone.\n(other) version: HCS2 (House committee substitute 2, 26 RS HB 534/HCS 2, saved 2026-11-11): carries slot d with the same Section 3 as the saved 'bill'. Against HCS 1 it deletes the voting-location copy-and-vote step and the words 'without presenting documentation verifying his or her United States citizenship', drops the 'cast vote record' section, and adds a KRS 118.405 section on a candidate's name appearing twice. The saved 'bill' (as passed House) differs from HCS 2 only in the title, which loses 'and declaring an emergency'.\n(other) version: HFA10 (House floor amendment 10 to HCS 2, saved 2026-11-11): touches ID. It deletes Section 3, the noncitizen check, in its entirety, so the bill would carry no slot d. The House voted not to suspend the rules to take it up (19-23).\n(other) version: HFA11 (House floor amendment 11 to HCS 2, saved 2026-11-11): no ID or citizenship text. It deletes Section 10 (KRS 118.405) and renumbers. Not taken up (19-20).\n(other) version: HFA12 (House floor amendment 12 to HCS 2, saved 2026-11-11): no ID or citizenship text. It deletes Section 1 (KRS 27A.070, felony-conviction notices) and renumbers. Not taken up (18-22).\n(other) note: Passed the House 53-40 on 2026-03-05, received in Senate 2026-03-06 and sent to the Committee on Committees (S); no later action recorded, so not enacted. The saved 'bill' text differs from orig_bill in a way that matters for coding slot d: the final House text has no database named, no monthly comparison and no 5-day removal. Section 3(4) allows documents at 'a voting location' as well as the clerk's office (the enrolled HB139 version allows only the clerk's office).",
